INT. CURLING CLUB - BAR - NIGHT

Wood paneling. A dartboard. Brooms hung on pegs. Through the frosted glass, the ice gleams under floodlights.

SKIP RAGNHILD "RAGS" ENGSTROM, 40s, tweed vest, nurses a ginger ale. Her third, TOBIAS "TOBY" ADEKUNLE, 30s, still in his team sweater, picks at a bowl of peanuts. Their lead, ELOISE "LOLO" BEAUMONT, 20s, shoes off, feet on a stool, scrolls her phone.

RAGS
Lolo, put the phone down.

LOLO
It's the final scores.

RAGS
We lost.

LOLO
By one stone.

TOBY
By one stone, Rags. One.

RAGS
Don't you start, Toby.

TOBY
I'm just saying, if you'd called the hammer on the ninth end --

RAGS
I called the hammer.

TOBY
You called it late.

Rags slams her glass on the bar. Lolo drops the phone. Toby freezes mid-peanut.

RAGS
I called it when the ice told me to.

LOLO
Rags, the ice doesn't talk.

RAGS
It does if you listen.

A silence. Toby slowly crunches a peanut. Lolo picks up her phone, slides it into her pocket. Rags unclenches her fist.

RAGS (CONT'D)
(quieter)
We did well, Lolo.

LOLO
We did.

TOBY
We did great, Rags.

RAGS
Don't suck up, Toby.

TOBY
I'm sucking up with respect.

Rags almost smiles. Lifts her glass.

RAGS
To the ninth end.

LOLO
To the ninth end.

TOBY
To the hammer.

They clink. Beyond the glass, the Zamboni hums across the ice, erasing everything.
